<section class="ip-section" id="access-control" aria-labelledby="access-control-heading">
  <div class="ip-section-heading">
    <h2 id="access-control-heading">The right information for each project role</h2>
    <p>Xinyinhai's project controls give people access according to the work they are approved to perform. Participation in a project does not provide unrestricted access to every file, sample or production resource.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="ip-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Project roles and information access">
    <table class="ip-table">
      <caption>Access is assigned and reviewed for the agreed project scope.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Project role</th><th scope="col">Information needed for the work</th><th scope="col">Access boundary</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Project coordination</th><td>Approved requirements, schedules, decisions and release records.</td><td>Coordinates distribution to the authorized participants and confirms requests for additional access.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Design and engineering</th><td>The design, electronic and firmware materials required for assigned development tasks.</td><td>Access follows the assigned discipline and approved development scope.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Production</th><td>Released work instructions, approved production files and the materials needed for the assigned operation.</td><td>Manufacturing access stays within the agreed production purpose; source files and keys are controlled separately.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Quality</th><td>Approved specifications, inspection criteria, test instructions and controlled samples.</td><td>Access supports inspection, validation and traceable quality records.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Approved external parties</th><td>The specific drawings, materials or samples approved for the subcontracted task.</td><td>Disclosure is limited to the authorized task and covered by the applicable confidentiality arrangements.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="ip-note">Project access is reviewed when responsibilities change, a person leaves the project or an external assignment ends. Permissions are adjusted or withdrawn, and controlled materials are returned, transferred or otherwise closed as agreed.</p>
</section>

<section class="ip-section" id="information-handling" aria-labelledby="information-handling-heading">
  <div class="ip-section-heading">
    <h2 id="information-handling-heading">Control the file, the version and the recipient</h2>
    <p>Customer information stays connected to its project, permitted use and approved version throughout development and manufacturing.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="ip-split">
    <div>
      <h3>Distribution and revision control</h3>
      <ul class="ip-checklist">
        <li>Identify the project, file purpose, revision and release status before distributing working or production documents.</li>
        <li>Confirm email recipients, attachments and disclosure scope before sending project information.</li>
        <li>Use the channels agreed for the material being transferred; detailed drawings, programs and keys require an agreed handover arrangement.</li>
        <li>Keep release and change records, and withdraw or clearly supersede obsolete working versions.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Terminals and removable storage</h3>
      <ul class="ip-checklist">
        <li>Limit access on project workstations and shared storage to authorized users and the information needed for their work.</li>
        <li>Control copying, exports and the use of removable storage rather than treating portable copies as unrestricted files.</li>
        <li>Apply the project rules when information moves between development, production, quality and an approved external party.</li>
        <li>Record agreed retention, return or disposal arrangements when a file set is replaced or the project closes.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="ip-section" id="people-access" aria-labelledby="people-access-heading">
  <div class="ip-section-heading">
    <h2 id="people-access-heading">Confidentiality follows the people doing the work</h2>
    <p>The requirements cover the actual development, production, quality and project personnel involved, together with approved external participants.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="ip-split">
    <div>
      <h3>Employees and project participants</h3>
      <p>Confidentiality training explains what information is controlled, who may receive it and how to report a concern. Project details, screens, drawings, prototypes and production work cannot be photographed, posted on social media or used for publicity without the required written authorization.</p>
      <p>Changes in a person's role trigger a review of file permissions, controlled items and outstanding handovers. Confidentiality obligations continue according to the agreed arrangements after participation ends.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Visitors and external personnel</h3>
      <p>Visits and external work are limited to approved purposes and areas. Access to controlled areas is managed, with accompaniment and entry records as required by the activity.</p>
      <p>Approved outside parties receive the confidentiality requirements relevant to their work. Further sharing, photography and the movement of project materials require authorization; approval for one task does not authorize unrelated use.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="ip-section" id="incident-response" aria-labelledby="incident-response-heading">
  <div class="ip-section-heading">
    <h2 id="incident-response-heading">A defined response when something is wrong</h2>
    <p>An unexpected recipient, missing item, unauthorized access or other confidentiality concern is treated as an exception to investigate and resolve.</p>
  </div>
  <ol class="ip-flow">
    <li><h3>Report and contain</h3><p>Record the concern, preserve relevant records and restrict further access or movement while the situation is assessed.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Investigate the scope</h3><p>Review the affected information or items, participants, versions, transfers and available traceability records.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Notify and coordinate</h3><p>Inform the responsible project contacts and notify the customer according to the agreed obligations and confirmed circumstances.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Correct and review</h3><p>Document corrective actions, verify their completion and use internal reviews to address the cause and update the relevant controls.</p></li>
  </ol>
</section>
